<h2>Question</h2>
<p>
  PR #982's round-2 <code>/review</code> raised two findings against a new statute anchor
  (<code>#fix-review-convergence-independent-root-cause</code>): it granted the loop clearing authority while
  conceding a precondition was absent, and left a related narrowing unbuilt and undisclosed. The reviewer asked
  for honesty about what is unbuilt. The author read that as "disclose in the anchor" and wrote the disclosure
  directly into the rule text — producing a <strong>655-word anchor</strong> (corpus median 324, p90 633) whose
  invariant 1 is a single <strong>277-word</strong> paragraph carrying build-status tokens ("today", "not yet",
  "build-pending", "still parks"). Round 3's simplicity lens then read the result as a layering violation: a
  ratified rule is supposed to be timeless, and this one goes false the moment the mechanization it describes
  ships. Decision <a href="/backlog/2854-does-point-in-time-build-status-belong-in-a-statute-anchor-o/">#2854</a>
  asks where point-in-time build status belongs — inside the anchor, or on the decision item / open guards that
  track it. Is there prior art for keeping a durable rule's text separate from its changing implementation
  status, and what does each shape of separation actually cost?
</p>

<h2>Recommendation — status lives on the item, not in the rule's sentences (Fork 1 option a)</h2>
<p>
  Three mature rule-documentation systems face this identical tension, and all three keep the rule and its status
  apart — the only variable is <em>how</em> they're kept apart, and that variable tracks implementation cost, not
  disagreement on the principle:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>ADR status field</strong> — status is a distinct structured field, never interleaved into the
  decision narrative.</li>
  <li><strong>IETF RFC + errata</strong> — the rule text is immutable; status and corrections live entirely in a
  separate, cross-linked system.</li>
  <li><strong>MDN / web-features Baseline badges</strong> — status is inlined, but only as an auto-generated
  render of a separate machine-readable dataset, never hand-authored prose.</li>
</ol>
<p>
  <strong>None of the three systems' core rule text carries hand-maintained "not yet built" prose inside the
  ruling itself.</strong> That is the shape PR #982 produced, and its absence from three independent, long-running
  systems is evidence against it, not a coincidence of scope.
</p>

<h2>Where status lives, across three systems</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>System</th><th>Rule/spec text</th><th>Where status lives</th><th>Status ever inline in the rule's own prose?</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>ADR (Nygard / MADR)</td><td>Decision + Consequences narrative, written once</td><td>A distinct <code>Status</code> field on the same record (proposed/accepted/deprecated/superseded)</td><td>No — status is a field, never merged into the narrative sentences</td></tr>
    <tr><td>IETF RFC + errata</td><td>Published RFC — immutable, never edited in place</td><td>Separate errata registry, cross-linked by RFC + section</td><td>No — the RFC text carries no "this is not yet implemented" caveat</td></tr>
    <tr><td>MDN / web-features Baseline</td><td>The feature/spec page</td><td>A badge auto-rendered from the <code>browser-compat-data</code> dataset</td><td>Visually yes, but the text is machine-generated from a separate source, not hand-authored</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Applying the three shapes to #2854's Fork 1</h2>
<p>
  The three systems map directly onto Fork 1's three options, and the mapping is what makes the recommendation
  concrete rather than a vibe:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Option (a)</strong> — item/guards track status, anchor states only the rule — is the
  <strong>RFC/errata</strong> pattern. The backlog is already the separate tracked system (memory rule #106,
  "Backlog Is The Tracker"), so this costs nothing new to adopt.</li>
  <li><strong>Option (b)</strong> — status inside the anchor, as PR #982 shipped — has <strong>no supporting
  precedent</strong> in any of the three systems, and produced the exact single-paragraph bloat the corpus
  measurement flagged.</li>
  <li><strong>Option (c)</strong> — a dedicated machine-readable field the gate parses and renders — is the
  <strong>MDN/Baseline</strong> pattern: the best reader experience, but only sound because a data pipeline (not
  prose) keeps the badge truthful. It needs the <code>/rules/</code> renderer to grow a parsed field, which
  #2854 already prices as highest-cost.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Read together, (a) and (c) are not rivals so much as adjacent points on one upgrade path: (a)'s
  anchor-links-to-item shape is exactly the structured pointer a later (c) renderer would parse <em>from</em>.
  Choosing (a) now does not foreclose (c) later — it is the cheap version of the same idea, not a different one.
</p>

<h2>Web-standards / platform alignment</h2>
<p>
  There is no W3C/WHATWG surface for statute-anchor authoring (it is internal governance process, not a browser
  standard), so this is a documentation-discipline question rather than a Web Everything standard design. The
  nearest platform-native analogue is the RFC/errata split itself — a citable spec text kept stable, with
  corrections layered on top rather than edited in — which is the same instinct behind treating
  <code>docs/agent/platform-decisions.md</code> as citable authority.
</p>

<h2>Confirmed in the wild, during this very prep</h2>
<p>
  The skeptic pass run against Fork 1's default surfaced a live instance of the predicted decay inside the very
  anchor under discussion: its invariant 1 still says the reviewer-id enforcement is "build-pending… still owed",
  while <code>scripts/lib/invariant-catalogue.json</code> has recorded that exact mechanism as
  <code>"status": "enforced"</code> (with real shipped code) since PR #1100 — the anchor's own prose is now false
  against ground truth the repo already tracks elsewhere. Separately, the anchor already needed a first round of
  stale-status correction (#2842, resolved) with a second still owed (#2853, open) within two weeks of being
  written. The pass also found a working, narrower version of option (c) already shipping —
  <code>invariant-catalogue.json</code>'s <code>status</code>/<code>owedTo</code>/<code>anchor</code> schema,
  gated by <code>validateInvariantEnforcers</code> (#2844) — for catalogued operational invariants specifically,
  not yet for the free-text tokens #2849 targets on uncatalogued prose. Full detail in the report.
</p>

<h2>How the research reshaped the fork</h2>
<p>
  Going in, Fork 1 was framed as three roughly-equal-weight options differentiated by cost alone. The survey
  sharpens that: (b) is not merely more expensive than (a), it is the one shape with <strong>no supporting
  precedent</strong> among systems that have run this exact tension at scale for decades, while (a) and (c) turn
  out to be the <em>same</em> shape at two different investment levels rather than competing designs — which is
  what lets (a) be recommended now without discarding (c) as a real future upgrade.
</p>
